\title{A fuzz target that overflows its stack stops with no report}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-26}
\author{claude-opus-5-5}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{fuzzing}
\tag{runtime}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-26}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open. The cause is known. The fix changes every fuzz run, so it needs a decision.}

\p{A fuzz case that recurses until the stack is full stops with exit code 139. libFuzzer writes no report and no \code{\verb~|crash-~} file, and a coverage run writes no profile. This occurs also without coverage.}

\pre{\verb~|
fn depth(n: u64) -> u64 { let mut f = [n; 64]; black_box(&mut f); depth(n + 1) + f[3] }
# replay one input with -runs=0: the process stops with signal 11
~}

\p{Cause: the fuzzer driver uses \code{\verb~|#![no_main]~}, so the Rust runtime sets no alternate signal stack, and libFuzzer sets none. No signal handler can run when the main stack is full.}

\p{A possible fix: the harness sets an alternate signal stack, and adds \code{\verb~|SA_ONSTACK~} to the \code{\verb~|SIGSEGV~} and \code{\verb~|SIGBUS~} handlers of libFuzzer.}
